            "content": [
              {
                "type": "output_text",
                "text": "The latest significant news is that NASA’s Perseverance rover has found evidence that a 245-foot-thick 75-meter stack of ancient rock on Jezero Crater’s rim was formed by repeated asteroid impacts. NASA/JPL reported on July 15, 2026, that this “Broom Point member” is likely more than 3.9 billion years old, making it among the oldest terrain ever examined by a Mars rover. Scientists say the rocks preserve a rare record of the early solar system’s violent impact history, a record that is mostly erased on Earth by plate tectonics【6:2†source】.\n\nAnother recent milestone: Perseverance has now driven the equivalent of a full marathon on Mars — 26.2 miles 42.195 km. It reached that mark on sol 1,890, after about five years and four months of driving, much faster than the Opportunity rover, which took 11 years and two months to cover the same distance【6:3†source】. A Mars Reconnaissance Orbiter HiRISE image captured Perseverance and its tracks from orbit just before the milestone, while the rover was operating west of Jezero Crater in an area nicknamed “Arbot”【6:3†source】.\n\nIn short: Perseverance is still active, still driving, and its newest science results are focused on very ancient rocks outside/along Jezero’s rim that may reveal how Mars — and the inner solar system — were shaped nearly 4 billion years ago.",
                "annotations": [
                  {
